sections.content:
Kako bi sve rečeno postalo praktično, Kevin Monette poziva vjenčane u dvorani da **obnove svoje predanje supružniku**. Vodit će ih kroz riječi koje su mnogi izgovorili na dan vjenčanja, a mnogi ih, kako primjećuje, od tada nisu ponovili.

Daje jasne upute:

- ako je supružnik prisutan, okrenite se prema njemu, uzmite njegove ruke u svoje i gledajte ga u oči;
- ako nije prisutan, zatvorite oči i zamislite da stoji pred vama;
- ako se nadate braku, izgovorite riječi svom budućem supružniku;
- ako ste posvećena osoba, izgovorite ih **Isusu**.

Zatim publika za njim i prevoditeljem ponavlja zavjet:

> „Ja, (ime), uzimam tebe, (ime), da budeš moja žena / moj muž. Obećavam ti vjernost u dobru i u zlu, u zdravlju i u bolesti. Ljubit ću te i poštovati sve dane svoga života.“

Tako večer koja je počela neispunjenim očekivanjima i prošla kroz najdublju krizu jednog braka završava ondje gdje je taj brak i započeo: kod izgovorenog *„da“*.

sections.content_en:
To make everything that was said practical, Kevin Monette invites married couples in the hall to **renew their commitment to their spouse**. He will guide them through the words that many spoke on their wedding day, and which many, as he observes, have not repeated since.

He gives clear instructions:

- if the spouse is present, turn toward them, take their hands in yours, and look them in the eyes;
- if not present, close your eyes and imagine they are standing before you;
- if you are hoping for marriage, speak the words to your future spouse;
- if you are a consecrated person, speak them to **Jesus**.

Then the audience repeats the vow after him and the translator:

> „I, (name), take you, (name), to be my wife / my husband. I promise to be faithful to you in good times and in bad, in sickness and in health. I will love you and honor you all the days of my life.“

Thus, an evening that began with unfulfilled expectations and went through the deepest crisis of a marriage ends where that marriage also began: at the spoken *„yes“*.
